// The assumption set an impl's generics contribute (issue #303) must never be
// consulted for a real type that merely shares a parameter's name.
//
// `Holder`'s own scope declares one parameter, `U`. Field `inner: T` is
// therefore the *struct* `T`, which holds a `String` and is not `Copy`, even
// though the impl below names its parameter `T` as well.
//
// This program was once accepted, because the assumptions were keyed by name
// and the field type `T` matched the entry meant for the parameter. They are
// keyed by type variable now — a parameter is not a named type — so the two
// cannot be confused whatever they are called.
//
// The companion positive case is in
// `evaluator/structs/97_copy_param_shadows_same_named_type.mtl`, where the
// substituted position genuinely is the parameter and shadowing must win.

struct T {
    s: String,
}

copy struct Holder<U> {
    inner: T,
    marker: U,
}

fun main() {}
